/* Generated by tools/build.js from content/lessons/project-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addLessons("project-plus", [{"t":"Project basics: projects vs operations and programs, the triple constraint, and project roles (sponsor, project manager, PMO, team, stakeholders)","hook":"It is your second week as a project coordinator at Riverbend Medical Group. Your inbox has three urgent messages. The network manager wants to know why the monthly patching cycle is not on your project list. The chief nursing officer has just asked for twelve more rooms on the nurse call rollout, but says the go-live date cannot move. And someone from the project management office wants your status report in their template by Friday. Each message assumes you know who decides what, which work counts as a project, and what happens when one person pulls on scope. Do you?","simple":"A project is a one-time job with a start and a finish that makes something new, like setting up a new office network. Work that repeats forever, like resetting passwords every day, is called operations. Every project has three linked limits: what you deliver (scope), how long it takes (time) and how much it costs (cost). Think of planning a family holiday. If you want to visit more cities, you need more days or more money, or the trip gets rushed and less enjoyable. Projects also have people with different jobs. The sponsor pays for the project and says yes to big decisions. The project manager runs the work each day. The team does the work. Stakeholders are everyone the project touches, even people who never come to a meeting.","body":["Start with the definition, because many exam questions turn on it. A project is a temporary effort with a defined start and end that creates a unique product, service or result. Moving a company's email to a cloud service, building a new branch office network or rolling out a new ticketing tool are projects. Running the help desk every day, patching servers each month and answering password-reset calls are operations: they are ongoing and repetitive, and they keep the business running. Temporary does not mean short; it means the effort ends when its objectives are met, or when it is clear they cannot or should not be met. A two-year data center build is still temporary. Projects often end by handing something over to operations, such as a new system the service desk will now support, which is why the two groups need to work together from the start rather than meeting for the first time at go-live.","Next, notice that projects can be grouped. A program is a set of related projects managed together because coordinating them brings benefits you would not get managing them separately, for example a digital workplace program with separate projects for laptops, collaboration tools and training. The program manager watches the dependencies between those projects and the combined benefit, such as making sure training does not start before the laptops arrive. A portfolio is the collection of all projects, programs and even some operational work an organization is funding, chosen and balanced to meet its strategy. The projects in a portfolio do not have to be related at all. Portfolio managers decide which efforts get money in the first place; project managers deliver the efforts that were chosen.","Every project is shaped by the triple constraint: scope (what will be delivered), time (the schedule) and cost (the budget). Quality sits in the middle, and many modern texts add risk and resources as further constraints. The key idea is that the constraints are linked, like the three sides of a triangle. If the sponsor adds scope, time or cost must usually grow, or quality suffers. If the budget is cut, scope or quality usually shrinks. If the deadline moves earlier, you need more money for extra people or you deliver less. When an exam question describes a change to one constraint, ask what happens to the others. Remember too that the project manager's job is to show those trade-offs clearly to the people who decide, not to absorb them silently by asking the team to work every weekend.","The Project+ exam expects you to know who does what. The sponsor champions the project, provides or secures funding, approves the charter and major changes, and helps remove organizational obstacles the project manager cannot move alone. The project manager plans, coordinates and controls the work day to day and is accountable for delivering the agreed objectives. The project management office (PMO) sets standards, templates, methods and governance for projects across the organization and may provide coaching, tools or portfolio reporting; it does not usually run each project directly. The project team does the work, often including subject matter experts (SMEs) who bring specialist knowledge. Stakeholders are anyone affected by or able to influence the project: users, customers, department heads, vendors and regulators.","A few supporting roles also appear on the exam. A project coordinator or project scheduler supports the project manager with documentation, meeting logistics, schedule updates and status tracking, but usually has less decision authority. A product owner, on agile projects, owns the backlog and represents the customer. A functional manager (the line manager of a department) owns people and may lend them to the project. Knowing whose authority applies is often the whole question: the sponsor approves funding, the functional manager releases staff, and the project manager directs project work.","Consider a worked example. A regional hospital wants to replace its nurse call system. The chief nursing officer, who asked for it and controls the budget, is the sponsor and signs the charter. The PMO provides the charter template and requires monthly status reports in its standard format. A project manager from IT is assigned, and a coordinator keeps the schedule and minutes. The team includes network engineers, the vendor's installers and two nurses acting as SMEs. Stakeholders include every ward manager, the facilities team, the service desk and the patients who press the buttons. When the vendor quotes more rooms than planned, the project manager does not quietly absorb the extra work. He shows the sponsor three options: more money, more time, or fewer rooms in phase one, and the sponsor chooses.","Watch for the common mistakes. Calling a monthly patch cycle a project is wrong because it repeats forever, so it is operations. Treating a program as just a big project misses the point that it is a group of related projects. Assuming the project manager approves the budget or the charter is wrong; the sponsor does. Thinking the PMO manages each project directly confuses setting standards with running work. Another frequent error is treating stakeholders as only the people in meetings. The service desk that will support the result and the users who will change how they work are stakeholders even if they never attend.","Finally, learn to read the scenario wording. 'Ongoing, repetitive work' points to operations; 'temporary, unique result' points to a project; 'several related projects coordinated for combined benefit' is a program; 'selected to meet strategic goals' is a portfolio. 'Who provides funding and signs the charter' is the sponsor. 'Who sets templates and standards across projects' is the PMO. 'The customer wants more features but the date is fixed' is a triple constraint question: the answer involves more cost, reduced quality or a formal trade-off decision made by the sponsor."],"analogy":"The triple constraint works like planning a road trip with a fixed car. Scope is how many towns you visit, time is how many days you have, and cost is your fuel and hotel money. Add towns and you need more days or more money, or you rush and enjoy each stop less, which is quality. The analogy stops working on roles: on a trip you might decide everything yourself, but on a project the sponsor makes the money and scope calls while the project manager plans the route and presents the options.","terms":[["Project","A temporary endeavor with a defined beginning and end that creates a unique product, service or result."],["Operations","Ongoing, repetitive work that keeps the business running, such as help desk support and routine patching."],["Program","A group of related projects managed in a coordinated way to obtain benefits not available from managing them separately."],["Portfolio","All the projects, programs and related work an organization funds, selected and balanced to meet strategic goals."],["Triple constraint","The linked limits of scope, time and cost, with quality at the center, where changing one affects the others."],["Sponsor","The senior person who champions the project, provides funding, signs the charter and approves major changes."],["Project management office (PMO)","The group that sets project standards, templates and governance across the organization."],["Functional manager","The line manager of a department who controls staff and may assign them to projects."]],"example":"A retailer launches a program to modernize its stores, with separate projects for new point-of-sale terminals, in-store Wi-Fi and staff training. The operations director sponsors the program and approves each project charter. The PMO provides a standard status report. When the Wi-Fi vendor's lead time slips by six weeks, the Wi-Fi project manager raises it, and the program manager moves the training project's dates so staff are not trained on a network that does not exist yet.","mistakes":[["Monthly patching is a project because it has a schedule and a checklist.","Having a schedule does not make work a project. Patching repeats indefinitely, so it is operations. A project is temporary and produces a unique result."],["The project manager approves the budget and signs the charter.","The sponsor provides funding and approves the charter and major changes. The project manager plans and runs the work and presents trade-offs to the sponsor."],["A program is simply a very large project.","A program is a group of related projects coordinated for combined benefits. Size has nothing to do with it; a portfolio, in turn, is all funded work, related or not."],["The PMO manages every project day to day.","The PMO sets standards, templates and governance and may coach or report on the portfolio. Day-to-day control belongs to each project manager."]],"tryit":[["You are managing a laptop refresh. The sponsor tells you the finance team now also needs docking stations and second monitors, but the budget is frozen and the end date is tied to a lease return. The team is already fully booked. What do you do?","Treat it as a triple constraint trade-off and take it to the sponsor rather than absorbing it. With cost and time fixed, the only honest options are to reduce other scope, accept a quality risk, or ask for a formal exception on budget or date. Document the options and their impacts and let the sponsor decide."],["A department head refuses to release her database administrator for your project, saying her team has an audit next month. You need him for two weeks. Who has the authority here, and who can help you?","The functional manager controls her staff, so you negotiate with her directly first. If you cannot agree, escalate to the sponsor, whose role includes removing organizational obstacles the project manager cannot move alone."]],"tip":"Separate the sponsor from the project manager: the sponsor funds, authorizes and approves major changes; the project manager plans and runs the work. Ongoing work is operations, not a project.","check":[["Is monthly server patching a project or operations?","Operations, because it is ongoing and repetitive rather than a temporary effort producing a unique result."],["The sponsor adds three new features but will not move the deadline. What is most likely to change?","Cost (more resources) or quality, because the triple constraint links scope, time and cost."],["What distinguishes a program from a portfolio?","A program groups related projects for coordinated benefits; a portfolio is all funded work selected to meet the organization's strategy, related or not."],["Who typically provides templates and governance standards for all projects?","The project management office (PMO)."]]},{"t":"Change control process: change requests, impact assessment, change control board, approval, implementation and communicating changes","hook":"It is Thursday afternoon at Northgate Insurance, and you are managing the customer portal rebuild. A developer pings you: the marketing director stopped by his desk this morning and asked for live chat on the login page. He has already started on it, because it looked like a two-day job and she seemed senior. Now the security lead wants to know why an unreviewed third-party script is in the test build, and the release date is six weeks out. Nothing about the chat feature appears in the scope statement, the budget or the risk register. How should this have gone, and what do you do right now?","simple":"Change control is a simple rule: nobody changes the agreed plan without asking first and thinking it through. If someone wants something new, they write it down. The project manager records the request, works out what it would cost in time, money and risk, and then the right people say yes, no or later. Only after a yes does the team do the work, and everyone is told the result. Think of a kitchen remodel. If you tell the builder halfway through that you want an extra window, a good builder writes it up, tells you it adds a week and some money, and waits for your signature before cutting into the wall. Change control is not about saying no. It is about making every yes a decision made with open eyes.","body":["Begin with why change control exists. Change is normal on projects: requirements become clearer, business priorities shift and problems appear. What matters is that changes are controlled. Change control is the formal process for proposing, evaluating, approving or rejecting and then implementing changes to the project's approved baselines for scope, schedule and cost. A baseline is the approved version of the plan that progress is measured against. Without change control, small untracked changes accumulate, the project drifts away from what was agreed, and nobody can explain later why it went over budget. Change control does not exist to say no; it exists to make sure every yes is a conscious decision with known consequences.","The process usually follows the same steps. First, someone identifies the need and submits a change request, a written description of what should change and why. A request can come from anyone: the sponsor, a user, a vendor or a team member. Second, the project manager logs it in the change log with an ID, date, requester and status so it can be tracked; a typical entry might read 'CR-014, 3 March, Marketing, add live chat, status: under assessment'. Third, the project manager and team perform an impact assessment: what the change will do to scope, schedule, cost, quality, resources and risk, and what happens if it is not made. Fourth, the change goes to the decision maker named in the change management plan, often a change control board (CCB) made up of the sponsor and key stakeholders. The CCB approves, rejects or defers it.","What happens after the decision matters just as much. If the change is approved, the project manager updates the affected baselines and documents, such as the scope statement, work breakdown structure (WBS), schedule, budget and risk register, and then implements the change like any other work. The change is validated to confirm it was delivered as approved. Finally, the decision is communicated to everyone affected, including the person who asked for it, using the channels in the communication plan. Rejected and deferred changes are also recorded, with the reason, so the same request does not keep reappearing and there is an audit trail an auditor or sponsor can follow later.","Authority levels are set in advance. Some organizations let the project manager approve small changes within agreed tolerances, for example up to two days of schedule impact or a small amount of money, and send larger ones to the CCB. The change management plan written during planning defines these thresholds, the forms or ticket types to use, and who sits on the board. Notice that the project manager rarely approves significant changes alone and never simply tells the team to start work on an unapproved request. Also separate the project CCB from the organization's change advisory board (CAB), which approves changes to production IT systems; a project deployment may need approval from both, one for the project scope change and one for touching live systems.","The order matters, and exam questions test it. The correct sequence is: identify and document the request, log it, assess impact, get a decision, update the plan and baselines, implement, validate, and communicate. A version of this can be pictured as a simple workflow.","```text\nRequest -> Log -> Impact assessment -> CCB decision\n  approved -> update baselines -> implement -> validate -> communicate\n  rejected/deferred -> record reason -> communicate\n```","Consider a worked example. Halfway through a customer portal project, the marketing director asks a developer to add a live chat feature. The developer redirects her to the project manager, who asks her to submit a change request. The impact assessment shows three extra weeks, a new software subscription and a security review. The CCB meets, weighs the benefit against the delay, and approves it for a second release instead of the current one. The project manager updates the release plan and backlog, logs the decision and emails the director and team explaining what was decided and why. The director did not get the feature this release, but she got a clear answer, a date and a reason.","Watch for the common mistakes. Implementing a change first and documenting it later is wrong outside a true emergency path. Skipping impact assessment because the change 'looks small' hides costs that add up. Letting the requester or the project manager approve beyond their authority breaks governance, and forgetting to communicate rejections leaves people assuming their request is in progress. Another trap is updating the schedule without updating the budget and risk register, which leaves baselines inconsistent.","Exam wording usually asks for the next step or the first thing to do. 'A stakeholder asks for a new feature' means the first step is a formal change request, not starting work or refusing outright. 'A change was requested; what next?' is impact assessment. 'Who decides?' is the CCB or the authority named in the change management plan. 'The change was approved; what next?' is update baselines, implement and communicate."],"analogy":"Change control is like ordering extra work from a builder during a home renovation. You ask for a change in writing, the builder prices it and says how many days it adds, and you sign before anyone picks up a hammer. If you say no, the builder notes that too, so nobody argues later about what was agreed. The analogy stops working on who decides: in your own house you sign alone, but on a project the decision belongs to the CCB or the authority in the change management plan, not to whoever asked.","mnemonic":"Really Lazy Admins Don't Update Inventory Very Carefully: Request, Log, Assess impact, Decide, Update baselines, Implement, Validate, Communicate.","terms":[["Change control","The formal process for evaluating and approving or rejecting changes to project baselines."],["Change request","A formal written proposal to modify scope, schedule, cost or another baseline."],["Change log","A record of all change requests with their status, decisions and dates."],["Impact assessment","An analysis of how a proposed change would affect scope, schedule, cost, quality, resources and risk."],["Change control board (CCB)","The group authorized to approve, reject or defer project change requests."],["Change management plan","The planning document defining the change process, forms, approval thresholds and board membership."],["Baseline","The approved version of the scope, schedule or cost plan, used to measure performance and changed only through change control."]],"example":"During an office relocation project, the facilities manager emails the project manager asking for two extra conference rooms to be wired. The project manager logs a change request, estimates the extra cabling, labor and a four-day delay, and presents it to the CCB. The board approves it, the project manager updates the scope statement, schedule and budget, and a note goes out to the cabling vendor, the facilities manager and the team.","mistakes":[["The change is small, so the team can just do it and update the paperwork later.","Outside a defined emergency path, work starts only after approval. Small changes still need logging and impact assessment, because many small changes are how projects drift over budget."],["The project manager approves all changes.","The project manager can approve only within tolerances set in the change management plan. Larger changes go to the CCB or another named authority."],["Rejected requests can simply be deleted.","Rejected and deferred requests are recorded with the reason and communicated to the requester. This keeps an audit trail and stops the request resurfacing without context."],["The project CCB and the IT change advisory board are the same thing.","The CCB decides on changes to project baselines. The change advisory board (CAB) approves changes to production systems. A deployment may need both."]],"tryit":[["A vendor emails you that the firewall model in your design is end-of-sale and offers a newer model at a higher price with a two-week lead time. Your change management plan lets you approve changes under three days and a small cost threshold; this exceeds both. What are your next steps, in order?","Log a change request for the hardware substitution, assess its impact on cost, schedule, configuration work and risk, and take it to the CCB because it exceeds your tolerance. If approved, update the budget, schedule and risk register, implement, validate the new model works as designed, and communicate the decision to the team and vendor."]],"tip":"When a question asks what to do first after someone requests a change, the answer is to document it as a formal change request and assess its impact, not to implement it or refuse it.","check":[["A user asks a developer directly for a new report. What should happen?","The request should be redirected into a formal change request, logged and assessed before any work begins."],["What is the step between logging a change request and the CCB decision?","The impact assessment on scope, schedule, cost, quality, resources and risk."],["Why record rejected change requests?","To keep an audit trail and stop the same request resurfacing without context."],["After a change is approved, what must the project manager update?","The affected baselines and documents, such as the scope statement, schedule, budget and risk register, and then communicate the decision."]]},{"t":"Types of change: scope creep, gold plating, timeline, budget, resource, requirements and emergency changes","hook":"You are three weeks from go-live on a warehouse scanning app for Cedar Lane Distribution. The schedule says you should be in final testing, but the build keeps changing. Floor supervisors have been asking the developer for 'just one more button', and he has been happy to help. A tester has built an animated dashboard nobody requested. This morning the security team announced a critical flaw in the scanner firmware and wants it patched before anything ships. Your sponsor asks a simple question: why are we behind, and what are we doing about it? Which of these is which kind of change, and how should each one be handled?","body":["Start with the big picture. Not all changes look the same, and the Project+ exam expects you to recognize the common types and how each should be handled. The idea behind all of them is the same: any change to what the project delivers, when it delivers or what it costs should pass through change control so the trade-offs are visible. What differs is where the change comes from, how urgent it is and which constraint it hits first."],"locked":true},{"t":"Risk management activities: identification, qualitative and quantitative analysis, risk register, probability and impact matrix","hook":"You are running a 300-laptop operating system upgrade for Elmwood County Schools. In the pilot, the accounting team's old payroll add-on refuses to launch. Nobody had written it down as a possibility, so there is no owner, no budget for a fix and no plan. The finance director asks you, a little sharply, what else is out there that nobody has thought about. You have a risk register template from the project management office, but it has been empty since kickoff. How do you find the other surprises before they find you, and how do you decide which ones deserve attention first?","body":["Begin with the definition, because the exam tests it constantly. A risk is an uncertain event or condition that, if it happens, affects at least one project objective. Most risks are threats, but some are opportunities, such as a vendor releasing a faster product early. Risk management means finding these uncertainties early and planning for them, instead of reacting when they turn into problems. Once a risk actually happens, it becomes an issue, which is tracked in the issue log and handled now rather than planned for. That distinction, risk as future and uncertain versus issue as present and certain, is one of the most tested ideas on the exam."],"locked":true},{"t":"Risk responses: avoid, mitigate, transfer, accept, escalate, exploit, enhance and share; contingency and management reserves","hook":"Your risk register for the Pinecrest Credit Union payroll replacement is finally full and scored. Now the steering committee wants to know what you are going to do about each red item, and how much money you are holding back. One risk is a brand-new database version nobody has run in production. Another is data conversion errors. A third is hardware failure in the first year. Meanwhile, the vendor offers early access to a feature that could halve training time. The chief financial officer asks whether buying a support contract makes the hardware risk go away. Does it, and which response fits each of the others?","body":["Start with what a good response looks like. Once risks are analyzed, each high-priority risk gets a planned response. The response should fit the size of the risk, be affordable, be owned by one person and be agreed in advance, so that when the trigger appears nobody has to invent a plan under pressure. A response that costs more than the risk it addresses is rarely worth it. The CompTIA Project+ exam expects you to match a scenario to the right response and to know which responses apply to threats and which to opportunities."],"locked":true},{"t":"Communication management: communication plan, synchronous vs asynchronous methods, audience, frequency and tailoring","hook":"It is 7 a.m. and your phone is buzzing. You are rolling out a new phone system for Meridian Freight across three offices, and last night's cutover in the smallest office slipped by a week. The regional director just found out from a frustrated receptionist, not from you. The engineers in another time zone are asking which version of the cutover runbook is current, because two copies are floating around in email. And the steering committee wants a status update, but last time they complained your report was twelve pages of technical detail. Nothing is technically broken. So why does this project suddenly feel like it is failing?","body":["Start with why this topic gets so much exam attention. Many project failures are really communication failures: the sponsor was surprised by a delay, users did not know a system was going down, or two teams worked from different versions of a document. Communication management is planning who needs what information, when, in what format and through which channel, and then making sure it happens. Project managers are often said to spend most of their time communicating. Good communication is also about listening: status meetings, surveys and one-on-one conversations tell you how stakeholders really see the project, which a report you send cannot."],"locked":true},{"t":"Stakeholder management: stakeholder register, power/interest grid, engagement and managing expectations","hook":"Two weeks before go-live of the new student information system at Hollis State College, a woman you have never met walks into your status meeting. She runs financial aid, her team processes thousands of records a term, and nobody asked them what they needed. She lists four requirements the design does not meet. Your sponsor, the provost, looks at you. Later that afternoon the registrar's staff, who have been loyal supporters, email to ask why the feature they were 'promised' is missing. You never promised it, but someone did. How did a project that followed its plan end up with this many unhappy people?","body":["Begin with a wide definition. A stakeholder is any person, group or organization that can affect, be affected by or believe they are affected by the project. For an IT project that includes the sponsor and team, but also end users, department managers, the service desk that will support the result, security and compliance teams, vendors, labor unions, and sometimes regulators or customers. Stakeholders you miss early tend to appear late with new requirements or objections, which is expensive because the design may already be built. A late requirement discovered in testing can cost far more to add than the same requirement discovered in planning."],"locked":true},{"t":"Resource management: resource types, allocation, leveling vs smoothing, shared resources and RACI charts","hook":"Priya is the only engineer at Bayview Logistics certified on the new firewall platform, and your rollout to 40 branches depends on her. You open the resource histogram for week six and see a bar at 70 hours: two branch cutovers and a design review all landed on her at once. She is also on the operations on-call rota that week. Her manager has already told you that outages come first. Meanwhile, two people both believe they own sign-off on the cutovers, and a third assumed someone else was telling the branches. Can you fix Priya's week without moving the end date, and who is actually in charge of what?","body":["Start with what counts as a resource. Resources are everything the project needs to do its work: people (human resources), equipment such as laptops and test servers, facilities such as a training room, materials, software licenses and money. Physical resources need ordering, storing and tracking; human resources need skills, availability and motivation. Resources can be dedicated to the project full time or shared with operations or other projects. Shared resources are a frequent source of conflict: a network engineer who also handles outages may be pulled away at the worst moment, so the project manager negotiates availability with functional managers and records it in a resource calendar that shows when each person or asset is available, including holidays, on-call weeks and maintenance windows."],"locked":true},{"t":"Schedule development: activity sequencing, dependencies (FS, SS, FF, SF), critical path, float, milestones, crashing and fast-tracking","hook":"The sponsor of the new Willow Creek Clinic branch network has just told you the opening date moved up: you have 15 days, not 18. Your engineers immediately offer to speed up the site survey, because it is the first thing on the list and they can finish it in half the time with an extra person. It sounds helpful. But when you look at the network diagram, you suspect that finishing the survey early will not move the opening day by even an hour. Why not, and where should the extra money and effort actually go?","body":["Start with how a schedule is built. A schedule turns the work breakdown structure (WBS) into dated activities. The project manager lists the activities needed to produce each work package, identifies milestones, estimates durations, then sequences them and finally assigns dates and resources. Sequencing is drawn as a network diagram, usually with the precedence diagramming method (PDM), where boxes (nodes) are activities and arrows are dependencies. The finished schedule is often shown as a Gantt chart, a bar chart of activities against a calendar, which is easier for stakeholders to read than the network diagram."],"locked":true},{"t":"Agile methodology: Scrum roles, events and artifacts, user stories, backlog refinement, velocity and Kanban","hook":"You have just joined Lantern Health's self-service portal team as a project coordinator. On day one, the security lead walks up to a developer mid-sprint and asks for a new login screen by Friday. The developer looks at the scrum master, who looks at you. Across the room, the IT operations team is drowning in unpredictable tickets and wonders whether two-week sprints even make sense for them. And a director has asked why your team's velocity is 28 when another team's is 45. Is the security lead's request allowed, is the director's comparison fair, and which way of working fits the operations team?","body":["Start with the agile mindset. Agile approaches deliver value in small, frequent increments and adapt as they learn, instead of fixing all requirements up front. They suit work where requirements are expected to change, such as new software or a customer-facing portal, and where users can give regular feedback. The Agile Manifesto values individuals and interactions over processes and tools, working software over comprehensive documentation, customer collaboration over contract negotiation, and responding to change over following a plan. The items on the right still matter; the items on the left matter more. That is why agile does not mean no plans or no documentation."],"locked":true},{"t":"Other methodologies: waterfall (predictive), hybrid, PRINCE2, Lean and Six Sigma (DMAIC), and choosing an approach","hook":"It is Monday morning at Harbor Credit Union, and you have just inherited three projects from a manager who left on Friday. One is a new server room with fixed electrical drawings and an inspector who signs off each stage. One is a mobile app that the marketing team keeps changing its mind about. The third is a loan process where one in eight applications bounces back with errors. Your director stops by your desk and asks a simple question: which way are you going to run each of these? You realize that running all three the same way would quietly doom at least two of them. So how do you choose?","body":["Agile is not the only way to run a project, and Project+ expects you to choose the right approach for the situation rather than defend a favorite. The starting point is waterfall, also called predictive or plan-driven. It moves through phases in order: requirements, design, build, test and deploy. Scope, schedule and cost are planned in detail up front and controlled against baselines, and each phase is usually signed off at a phase gate, a formal review where approval is needed before the next phase starts. Waterfall suits projects with stable, well-understood requirements, fixed-price contracts, heavy regulation or physical work that is hard to change later, such as cabling a building or installing a data center. Its weakness is that users may not see the product until late, when changes are expensive. Waterfall does not forbid change; it controls change through a formal change control process so the baselines stay meaningful."],"locked":true},{"t":"Team dynamics and conflict resolution: team development stages, conflict techniques, motivation and running effective meetings","hook":"The weekly status meeting at Pinecrest Logistics was supposed to take thirty minutes. It is now minute fifty-two. Dev, the security engineer, and Lena, the network lead, are on their third round of the same argument about a legacy protocol, and everyone else has stopped taking notes. Two people are answering email. The vendor on the call has gone quiet. After the meeting, a junior technician messages you privately: 'Is this project going to fall apart?' You are the project manager. You could pick a winner, change the subject, or let it burn out. Which response actually fixes the team, and which only makes the next meeting worse?","body":["Projects are delivered by people, and new teams go through predictable stages. Bruce Tuckman's model describes them: forming, when members are polite, unsure of their roles and dependent on the leader; storming, when disagreements over roles, approaches and priorities surface; norming, when the team agrees on ways of working and builds trust; performing, when the team works effectively with little supervision; and adjourning, when the work ends and the team disbands. Teams can slip back, for example into storming when a new member joins or priorities change, so the stages are not a one-way ladder. The project manager adapts their style to the stage: more direction when forming, facilitation and coaching while storming, support when norming, and delegation when performing. Adjourning deserves attention too, because recognizing contributions and capturing lessons learned helps people leave well."],"locked":true},{"t":"Discovery and concept preparation: business case, feasibility, ROI and payback period, and preliminary scope","hook":"The chief financial officer of Maple Ridge Health is holding your one-page proposal for a self-service password tool. She has seven other requests on her desk and money for maybe two of them. 'You say this will save us time,' she says, tapping the page. 'How much, how soon, and what happens if we just do nothing?' You realize your proposal describes the tool beautifully but never answers those three questions. Without them, it will lose to the request from facilities that came with numbers. What does a proposal need before anyone should spend a dollar on it?","body":["Before a project exists, someone has an idea or a problem: the file server is out of space, customers want online ordering, a regulation requires new controls. The discovery or concept preparation phase decides whether that idea is worth turning into a project at all. Project+ treats it as the first phase of the life cycle, before initiating, and it matters because the cheapest time to stop a bad idea is before any money has been committed. Work in this phase is deliberately light: enough analysis to support a decision, not a full plan."],"locked":true},{"t":"Initiating: project charter, sponsor authorization, high-level scope, assumptions, constraints and success criteria","hook":"Three weeks into the new warehouse scanner rollout at Cedar Freight, a functional manager refuses to release two of her technicians to your project. 'Who said this was a priority?' she asks. 'And who said you could book my people?' You start to explain that everyone knows the project was approved. She asks to see it in writing. You search your inbox and find a hallway conversation turned into a two-line email from the operations director, nothing more. No signed document, no stated budget, no named project manager. Suddenly you are not sure what authority you actually have. What should have existed before you booked a single person?","body":["Initiating turns an approved idea into an official project. Its most important output is the project charter, a short document that formally authorizes the project, names the project manager and gives them authority to apply organizational resources to the work. The sponsor, or another person with authority outside the project, signs it. The project manager may help draft the charter, but they do not sign their own authorization, just as an employee does not approve their own expense report. Until the charter is signed, the project does not formally exist, and a project manager who starts spending money or booking people without one is taking a real risk."],"locked":true},{"t":"Initiating: identifying stakeholders, assigning the project manager, and holding the kickoff meeting","hook":"Six weeks into the online permits project for the Town of Willow Bend, a fire marshal you have never met walks into your design review. 'Nobody asked us,' he says, 'but every commercial permit over a certain size has to come through my office first. Your workflow skips us entirely.' The room goes quiet. Your requirements are signed, your developers are halfway through the build, and the kickoff slides proudly listed every department that mattered, except this one. You think back to the first week of the project and wonder: where exactly did this person fall through the cracks, and how could you have found him on day one?","body":["Alongside the charter, initiating identifies the people involved, starting with the project manager. The project manager is assigned as early as possible, ideally while the charter is being written, so they understand the reasoning behind the project and can help shape it. The charter records their authority: for example, whether they can approve spending up to a limit, choose team members or approve small changes. A project manager assigned only after planning is finished inherits commitments, dates and budgets they never agreed to, and has little credibility when defending them."],"locked":true},{"t":"Planning: requirements gathering, scope statement, work breakdown structure (WBS) and WBS dictionary","hook":"It is go-live week for the new intranet at Bluewater Insurance. The search works, the pages look clean, and the demo for leadership went perfectly. Then the legal team asks where the retention hold feature is, the one that stops documents under litigation from being deleted. Nobody can find it in the requirements. The marketing director asks why the mobile app is missing, and your developer insists it was never in scope. Two angry emails, two different problems: one requirement that was never captured, and one that was never ruled out. Which documents, built weeks ago, would have prevented both arguments?","body":["Planning turns the charter's high-level view into a plan the team can follow, and scope planning comes first because everything else, the schedule, budget and risk register, is built on it. It begins with requirements: the conditions or capabilities the product must have. Business requirements describe why the organization needs it ('reduce password-reset calls by half'). Functional requirements describe what the product must do ('users can reset their own password'). Non-functional requirements describe qualities such as performance, availability, security, usability and accessibility ('the reset page loads in under two seconds and supports multifactor authentication'). Missing non-functional requirements are a classic cause of systems that work in a demo but fail in production, because a demo with five users never tests load, security or recovery."],"locked":true},{"t":"Planning: estimating time and cost (analogous, parametric, three-point/PERT, bottom-up) and setting baselines","hook":"The steering committee at Granite Valley Schools wants one number: how long will it take to roll out the new device management agent to every staff laptop? Your lead technician says 'about six weeks, probably.' Another engineer, who has been burned before, says 'could be three months if the integration fights us.' The superintendent is already writing 'six weeks' in her newsletter draft. You know that once a single date is printed, it will become a promise. You also know you have numbers from last year's rollout and a pilot starting next week. How do you turn guesses into an estimate you can defend, and then lock it in as something to measure against?","body":["Once work packages and activities are known, the team estimates how long each will take and what it will cost. Estimates are best made by the people who will do the work, because they know the details and are more committed to numbers they helped create. Every estimate should state its assumptions and a range, because every estimate is uncertain. Accuracy usually improves as the project progresses, which is why early estimates are rough (a rough order of magnitude, or ROM) and later ones are refined into budget and definitive estimates. This refining as information arrives is called progressive elaboration. Present an estimate as a range, such as 8 to 10 weeks, rather than a single date whenever you can, so stakeholders understand the uncertainty instead of treating a guess as a promise."],"locked":true},{"t":"Planning: budget development and procurement (make vs buy, RFI, RFP, RFQ, statement of work, contract types)","hook":"The invoice on your screen from the contractor helping Elmwood Library migrate its catalog is three times what you budgeted. You call the vendor's account manager. She is polite and unbothered: the contract is time and materials, there is no cap, and your team kept asking for 'just one more thing' by email. Meanwhile, finance has asked why the new cloud subscription was filed as a capital purchase, and procurement wants to know why a request for quote went out for a system nobody had designed yet. Three problems, all rooted in decisions made months ago in planning. Which choices would have prevented each one?","body":["The project budget is built by adding up the cost estimates for activities and work packages, then adding the contingency reserve for identified risks. That total, spread over time, is the cost baseline. It is often drawn as an S-curve, because spending starts slowly during planning, speeds up during execution when most people and purchases are active, and tails off at the end. The management reserve for unknown risks is added on top to form the total project budget, but it is held by management and released only with their approval. Budgets include labor, hardware, software licenses, cloud subscriptions, facilities, training, travel and vendor costs."],"locked":true},{"t":"Planning: subsidiary plans for quality, risk, communication, transition and release","hook":"It is 7:40 on the Monday morning after go-live of Summit Transit's new expense system, and the service desk queue already has ninety tickets. The analysts have never seen the new screens. There are no runbooks. The vendor's developers rolled off the project on Friday. When a supervisor asks whether the old system can be switched back on, nobody knows if that was ever planned, or who would decide. You are standing beside the service desk manager as her phone keeps ringing, and she asks the question you were hoping to avoid: 'Who was supposed to get us ready for this?' Which plans should have answered her weeks ago?","body":["The project management plan is not one document but a collection: the baselines for scope, schedule and cost plus subsidiary plans that describe how each area will be managed. On a small project each subsidiary plan may be a page; on a large one it may be substantial. Writing them forces the team to think through how the project will run before problems arise, and it gives new team members and auditors a single place to see how decisions are made. Like the baselines, the plan is approved and then updated through change control, so it stays a reliable reference rather than a forgotten draft."],"locked":true},{"t":"Executing: directing the work, managing vendors, quality assurance vs quality control, and deliverable acceptance","hook":"An invoice lands in your inbox at Brookfield Community Bank: the vendor implementing your new customer relationship system is billing for the data migration milestone. Their email says 'all tests passed.' But the sales director has not seen the migrated data yet, and last week a process audit found the vendor's developers had quietly stopped doing code reviews. Then you notice a line item for a reporting feature that someone on your team asked for in a casual email. Your finger hovers over the approve button. Passing tests, a skipped process, an informal request and an unsigned deliverable: which of these should stop you from paying today?","body":["Executing is where most of the project's time and money are spent. The project manager directs and manages the work defined in the plan: assigning tasks, coordinating teams and vendors, running meetings, removing obstacles, developing the team, acquiring any remaining resources and keeping stakeholders engaged. Approved changes are implemented, issues are logged in the issue log with an owner and resolved, and work performance data, meaning what was actually done, when and at what cost, is collected so monitoring and controlling can compare it with the baselines. Executing and monitoring run side by side; the plan is carried out and checked at the same time."],"locked":true},{"t":"Monitoring and controlling: tracking progress against baselines, variance analysis, issue management and escalation","hook":"It is Thursday morning at Lakeview Regional Library, and you open the weekly status dashboard for the branch network upgrade. The schedule bar for site three has turned amber, the vendor's shipment confirmation still has not arrived, and the facilities manager has just emailed asking whether her electricians can have the server room next Tuesday, the same day your installers are booked. Your sponsor will ask at Friday's steering meeting whether the project is on track. You could quietly push some dates in the tool and hope nobody notices. Or you could measure how far off the plan you really are, decide what is yours to fix, and know exactly when to raise your hand. Which is it, and how do you tell the difference?","body":["Monitoring and controlling is not a phase that waits until execution is over. It runs alongside execution for the whole life of the project. The project manager compares actual performance with the approved baselines for scope, schedule and cost, identifies variances, decides on corrective or preventive actions, and makes sure that any change to the plan itself goes through change control. Without it, a team can be very busy and still be heading in the wrong direction, and nobody notices until the deadline or the budget is already gone."],"locked":true},{"t":"Closing: formal acceptance, transition to operations, contract closure, releasing resources, lessons learned and archiving","hook":"Six weeks after the new visitor management system went live at Cedar Valley Hospital, you get a puzzled call from the finance office. The implementation vendor has sent another invoice, three contractor accounts are still active, and nobody in the service desk knows who to call when the badge printers jam. The project team was reassigned the day after go-live, and the person who knew the printer workaround has moved to another region. Technically the system works. So why does it feel like the project never really ended, and what should have happened in those last few weeks to close it cleanly?","body":["Closing formally ends a project or a phase, and it is the last stage of the project life cycle that Project+ expects you to recognize. It happens when all the work is done and accepted, but also when a project is cancelled or terminated early. In both cases an orderly close protects the organization: it records what was achieved, frees people and money for other work, and preserves knowledge for future projects. Skipping closure leaves loose ends such as open contracts, unpaid or disputed invoices, unused licenses, active accounts nobody owns and unanswered questions about who supports the new system."],"locked":true},{"t":"Project management software: scheduling tools, Gantt charts, Kanban boards, ticketing systems and dashboards","hook":"You have just inherited the student portal rebuild at Northfield Community College. The previous project manager left behind a scheduling file with 400 tasks, a Kanban board nobody has touched in a week, a ticket queue full of untriaged defects and a dashboard that still shows everything green. The dean emails asking for a quick update before her budget meeting, the developers want to know which bugs to fix first, and you need to find out whether the launch date is still realistic. Every one of these tools can help, but each answers a different question. Which one do you open first, and what do you show to whom?","body":["Project managers rely on software to plan work, track it and report on it. The exam does not test particular products; it tests whether you know what each type of tool is for and which view answers which question. The main categories are scheduling tools, Gantt charts, Kanban boards, ticketing systems and dashboards, and many modern platforms combine several of them."],"locked":true},{"t":"Collaboration tools: real-time vs asynchronous communication, shared workspaces, file sharing and document version control","hook":"The cloud migration team at Brightwater Logistics spans three cities and two continents. On Monday you discover that the database team has been building against version 3 of the design document, which was emailed in March, while the network team is working from version 5 in the shared folder. A key decision about firewall rules was made on a late-night video call that half the team could not attend, and nobody wrote it down. Meanwhile, a contractor has posted the network diagram in a public link to share it faster. The tools are all there, so why is everyone confused, and which tool should carry which kind of conversation?","body":["Modern project teams are often spread across offices, homes and time zones, so collaboration tools are central to getting work done. The Project+ exam groups these tools by how they are used rather than by product names, so focus on the categories: real-time communication, asynchronous communication, shared workspaces and file sharing, and document version control. The skill being tested is choosing the right tool for the situation."],"locked":true},{"t":"Quality charts: Pareto, histogram, run and control charts, scatter diagrams and fishbone (Ishikawa) diagrams","hook":"It is the monthly service review at Pinecrest County Schools, three weeks after 2,000 new student laptops were deployed. The help desk lead slides a spreadsheet of 600 tickets across the table and says the rollout was a disaster. The vendor says the numbers are normal for a launch. The superintendent wants to know what to fix first and whether things are getting better or worse. Everyone has an opinion, and nobody has a picture. You have the data. Which chart turns that spreadsheet into an answer each person in the room will accept?","body":["Quality tools help a team understand problems using data instead of opinions, and they are used in quality control activities while the project is executing as well as in planning how quality will be measured. On the exam you will usually be given a situation, or a description of a chart, and asked which tool fits. So for each chart, learn the question it answers: how values are distributed, which causes matter most, whether a process is stable over time, whether two variables are related, or what might be causing a problem."],"locked":true},{"t":"Agile reporting: burndown and burnup charts, velocity charts and cumulative flow diagrams","hook":"Sprint review day at Riverbend Credit Union's mobile app project. The product owner frowns at the burndown on the wall: the line has been flat for four days. Then the business sponsor asks why the release date has moved for the third time when the team 'seems to be working hard'. Another manager suggests the team should match the velocity of the web team down the hall, which completes twice as many points per sprint. You have four charts in the team's tool that could answer all of this. Which chart tells which story, and which suggestion should you push back on?","body":["Agile teams report progress with simple visual charts rather than long written status reports. These charts are often called information radiators, because they sit where everyone can see them, on a wall or a shared screen, and 'radiate' the team's status without anyone having to ask. They are updated frequently, usually daily, straight from the team's board or tool, so they show reality with very little extra effort. The exam expects you to read four of them: burndown, burnup, velocity and the cumulative flow diagram."],"locked":true},{"t":"Earned value management: PV, EV, AC, CV, SV, CPI, SPI, EAC and ETC, and interpreting the results","hook":"Halfway through the warehouse scanner rollout at Summit Supply Co., the finance director stops you in the hallway. 'You have spent exactly half the budget at the halfway point,' she says. 'So we are on track, right?' You are not sure. The installers have finished fewer sites than planned, and two of the sites they did finish needed rework. Spending half the money is only good news if half the value has been delivered. Before Friday's review, you need numbers that combine scope, schedule and cost into one honest picture. How do you work out where the project really stands and where it is likely to end up?","body":["Earned value management (EVM) combines scope, schedule and cost into one set of measures. Looking only at money spent tells you very little: spending half the budget is fine if half the work is done, and alarming if only a quarter is. EVM answers the real questions: are we getting the planned value for our money, and are we doing the work as fast as planned? It needs three numbers measured at the same point in time, plus the total budget. Those numbers come from the project's cost baseline, the schedule and the team's progress reports, so EVM is only as accurate as the percent-complete estimates behind it."],"locked":true},{"t":"Core project documents: project management plan, schedule, RACI, risk register, issue log, change log and assumption log","hook":"Your first morning as project manager on the phone system replacement at Willow Creek Insurance. The sponsor wants to know why the go-live date in her calendar does not match the one the team quotes. A network engineer insists the firewall design is 'not his job'. Someone mentions that the vendor is late with handsets again, and a business analyst asks whether anyone ever confirmed that every office has the same cabling. Each of these questions has an answer, and each answer lives in a specific project document. Do you know which one to open for each, and which one to update next?","body":["Project+ expects you to know which document holds which information, because many exam questions describe a need and ask where to look or what to update. Project documents fall into two broad groups. Plans describe how the project will be run and what the targets are. Logs and registers track what is actually happening and are updated continuously. Knowing the difference, and knowing which log owns which kind of entry, lets you answer quickly."],"locked":true},{"t":"Meeting and status documentation: agendas, minutes, action items, status reports and executive summaries","hook":"The weekly project meeting for the classroom technology refresh at Maple Grove School District has run 30 minutes over for the third week in a row. The same argument about projector models comes up every time, because nobody can remember what was decided. Two tasks fell through the cracks because they were assigned to 'the team'. Meanwhile, the superintendent admits she stopped reading your 18-page status pack a month ago. Everyone is busy and well meaning, but the information is not landing. What would change if the right document reached the right people at the right time?","body":["A large part of a project manager's job is keeping people informed, and much of that happens through meetings and reports. Good documentation makes meetings shorter, decisions clearer and follow-up reliable. Poor documentation leads to repeated discussions, forgotten tasks and arguments about what was agreed. The exam tests whether you know which document is used when, what each should contain and how to tailor reporting to different audiences."],"locked":true},{"t":"Vendor and procurement documents: NDA, MOU, SLA, SOW, purchase orders, invoices and change orders","hook":"Your team at Oakridge Medical Group is choosing a vendor to run 24-hour monitoring of the clinic network. A shortlisted vendor's sales engineer asks for your full network diagram 'just to size the quote'. Procurement wants to know whether you need a purchase order or a change order, the vendor's draft contract talks about 'service credits', and a partner university has sent a non-binding memorandum about sharing a training lab. You are not a lawyer, and nobody expects you to be. But if you send the wrong document, or skip one, the organization could be exposed. Which paperwork belongs at which step?","body":["IT projects rarely deliver everything with internal staff. Hardware, software, cloud services and specialist skills are bought from vendors, so project managers regularly handle vendor documents. You are not expected to be a lawyer; legal and procurement teams draft and approve contracts. But you do need to know what each document does, when it is used and which one fits a situation, because using the wrong one can leave the organization unprotected."],"locked":true},{"t":"Decision-making tools: SWOT, cost-benefit analysis, decision trees and expected monetary value, brainstorming and root cause analysis","hook":"It is Monday afternoon at Pinewood Logistics, and the sponsor of your warehouse database migration wants an answer by Friday: do the migration with your own staff, or pay a specialist vendor. Priya from infrastructure insists the team can handle it. Marcus from finance points out that the vendor quote is fifteen thousand dollars higher. Meanwhile the shipping app has crashed three times this month, and each time someone simply restarted the server. Everyone has an opinion, and the loudest voice in the room is winning. You need a way to turn opinions into a choice you can explain and defend later. Which tools do you reach for, and how do you show the sponsor the numbers instead of the noise?","body":["Projects involve a constant stream of decisions: which option to fund, which vendor to pick, how to respond to a risk, why a problem keeps coming back. Structured decision tools make those choices more objective, easier to explain to stakeholders and easier to defend later, for example when an auditor or a new sponsor asks why the team chose one path over another. The exam tests whether you can match each tool to its purpose and, for expected monetary value, do the arithmetic. As you read, notice that each tool answers a different question, and that question is the clue the exam gives you."],"locked":true},{"t":"Security concepts for IT projects: CIA triad, least privilege, access reviews and building security requirements in from the start","hook":"It is the second week of the customer portal project at Harbor Credit Union, and the contract developer, Leo, sends you a friendly message: could someone just share the team admin password so he can get started today? The go-live date is already printed on the marketing calendar, and the security team has not been invited to a single meeting yet. Sharing one password would save an afternoon. It might also mean that months from now, when an auditor asks who changed a customer record, nobody can answer. What access should Leo actually get, and when should security have joined this project?","body":["Every IT project touches security, even when security is not its goal. A new application may store personal data, a network upgrade may open new paths into the company, and a vendor may need access to internal systems. Project+ does not expect deep technical security skills, but it does expect a project manager to recognize security needs, involve the security team early and plan the work and time that security requires. In practice, that means security shows up in the stakeholder register, the requirements, the schedule, the risk register and the go-live checklist, not just in a late-stage review."],"locked":true},{"t":"Data privacy and compliance: PII, PHI, data classification, retention, GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS and audits","hook":"You are three weeks into replacing the patient scheduling system at Riverbend Family Clinics, and the lead developer, Sam, has a plan to speed up testing: copy last month's production database into the test environment so the data looks realistic. In the same meeting, the vendor mentions their hosting is in a region nobody has checked, and the clinic's privacy officer is not on your stakeholder list at all. Nothing has gone wrong yet. But you can already picture an auditor asking where patient records went and who approved it. What rules apply to this data, and what does your plan need before anyone copies a single record?","body":["Many IT projects collect, move or store data about people, and that brings legal, regulatory and contractual obligations. A project manager does not need to be a lawyer, but must recognize regulated data when it appears, involve the privacy and compliance teams early, and plan the extra requirements, reviews and time that compliance adds. Getting this wrong can lead to fines, breach notifications, lost customer trust and a system that cannot go live. The good news is that most of the work is predictable once you know what to look for."],"locked":true},{"t":"IT infrastructure basics: servers, networks, storage and endpoints, and how infrastructure changes affect projects","hook":"The sponsor at Maple Grove Insurance wants the new document management system live in three months, and she has already told the board. You walk into your first meeting with Dana, the infrastructure lead, expecting a quick hello. Instead she lists, calmly, that new servers take about eight weeks to arrive, the storage array is nearly full, the vendor needs a firewall rule for remote support, and the only maintenance windows are on Sunday mornings. None of this is on your schedule. Your date suddenly looks fragile. What do you need to understand about infrastructure to build a plan that will actually hold?","body":["A project manager does not need to configure a router or build a server, but they do need to understand the parts of IT infrastructure well enough to plan the work, ask the right questions, estimate realistically and see how changes ripple across the organization. Many project delays come from infrastructure surprises: hardware that takes weeks to arrive, a firewall change nobody requested, or a storage system that is already full. This lesson walks through the main building blocks and then the ways they affect your schedule, budget and risks."],"locked":true},{"t":"Cloud and deployment models: on-premises, IaaS, PaaS, SaaS, public, private and hybrid cloud, and their trade-offs","hook":"The email servers at Cedar Valley Community College are five years old and the vendor has announced the end of support. In the planning meeting, Ray from IT wants to buy new servers, Lena from finance asks why the college would spend a large sum up front when a monthly subscription exists, and the registrar wants to know who will be blamed if student emails leak. Someone says 'just move it to the cloud', as if that ends the conversation. You know it is really the start of one. Which cloud model fits, who is responsible for what, and how does the choice change the budget and the plan?","body":["Many projects today involve the cloud, either moving existing systems there or adopting new cloud services. The model chosen changes the work to be done, the costs and how they are paid, the risks, and who is responsible for what. The exam tests the service models, the deployment models and the trade-offs a project manager must plan for. Think of two separate questions: what does the provider manage for us (the service model), and who shares the cloud and where does it run (the deployment model)."],"locked":true},{"t":"Software development life cycle and DevOps: CI/CD, development, test, staging and production environments, and release management","hook":"It is Friday at 4:30 p.m. at Northgate Savings, and a developer, Omar, wants to push a small pricing change straight to the live mobile banking app. 'It passed the unit tests,' he says, 'and the pipeline is automated anyway.' The release notes are blank, nobody from the business has looked at it, and you are not sure what would happen if it broke at the weekend. You have heard the words CI, CD, staging and release management in every stand-up for months. Now you need to know what they actually protect you from, and whether this change should go live tonight.","body":["When a project builds or changes software, it follows a software development life cycle (SDLC), a structured sequence of activities from idea to retirement. A common form has these phases: planning, requirements analysis, design, development (coding), testing, deployment and maintenance. The SDLC can be run in a waterfall style, with each phase completed before the next starts, or iteratively and incrementally as in agile, where each sprint includes a little requirements work, design, coding and testing. Knowing the phases helps a project manager plan the right activities, reviews and sign-offs, such as a requirements sign-off before heavy design work in a waterfall project or a sprint review at the end of each iteration in agile."],"locked":true},{"t":"Operational change management: change advisory board, maintenance windows, change freezes, rollback plans and downtime","hook":"Your payroll database migration at Summit Health Partners is ready, the sponsor has approved the project change log, and the team wants to cut over this Thursday night. Then Grace from IT operations calls. There is already a network change booked for Thursday, month-end payroll runs the following week, and nobody has seen a rollback plan. 'Your sponsor approved the project,' she says, 'but she does not approve changes to production. The CAB does.' You thought the approvals were done. Who actually decides whether your change can touch the live environment, and what do they need to see?","body":["Project change control manages changes to the project's plan: its scope, schedule and budget. Operational change management manages changes to the organization's live IT environment, whether they come from projects or from daily operations. Any project that deploys anything into production must follow it. Ignoring it is a quick way to cause outages, collide with other teams' changes and lose the trust of the operations staff who will support your system after you leave. The two processes have different approvers and different purposes, and the exam expects you to keep them apart."],"locked":true},{"t":"Physical and environmental considerations: facility access, power and cooling, asset tracking and secure disposal","hook":"The server refresh at Bayside Freight is going well until Tuesday afternoon, when two of the shiny new servers shut themselves down. The room is warm, the backup power unit is beeping, and a contractor you do not recognize is wandering near the racks with an expired badge. Meanwhile, facilities asks what to do with forty old servers stacked in the hallway, and someone suggests just wiping the drives and donating them to a local school. None of this was in the project plan. Which physical and environmental tasks did the plan miss, and how do you put them back before anything worse happens?","body":["IT projects do not happen only in software. Office moves, data center work, new network closets, hardware refreshes and branch openings all involve physical spaces, and those bring their own planning needs, risks and dependencies on facilities teams, landlords, electricians and security staff. The exam expects you to recognize these considerations and include them in the plan, not discover them on installation day. A physical site survey early in planning, walking the space with facilities, is often the cheapest risk reduction a project can buy."],"locked":true},{"t":"Governance: PMO standards, phase gate reviews, organizational change management, training and user adoption","hook":"The new expense system at Lakeshore Engineering went live on time and under budget, and the vendor has already sent a congratulations email. Six weeks later, the finance director stops you in the hallway. Half the managers are still emailing spreadsheets, the help desk is buried in 'how do I' tickets, and the steering committee wants to know why the project's success report says 'complete' when nobody seems to be using the system. You followed the plan. What did the plan leave out, and which governance checkpoint should have caught it before full rollout?","body":["Governance is the framework of rules, roles and decision processes that keeps projects aligned with organizational goals and run consistently. It answers questions such as who may approve spending, which documents every project must have, who decides when priorities conflict, and when a project must be reviewed before it continues. Good governance is not bureaucracy for its own sake; it protects the organization's money and makes sure effort goes to the work that matters most. For a project manager, governance shows up as templates to use, approvals to obtain and reviews to prepare for."],"locked":true},{"t":"Business continuity for IT projects: backups, disaster recovery, RTO and RPO, and testing before go-live","hook":"The go/no-go meeting for the new online ordering system at Oakridge Bakery Supply is tomorrow morning. Functional testing passed, users are trained and the marketing email is scheduled. Then Nadia, the operations manager, asks a quiet question: 'If the database dies at noon on our busiest day, how long until we are taking orders again, and how many orders do we lose?' The room goes silent. Backups are configured, someone says, but nobody has ever restored one. Is the system really ready, and what would you need to show Nadia before you could honestly say yes?","body":["When a project delivers a new system, the organization will soon depend on it. Business continuity planning (BCP) makes sure the business can keep operating if that system or the site it runs in fails. Disaster recovery (DR) is the technical part of that: how IT restores systems and data after an outage, a cyberattack such as ransomware, which encrypts data and demands payment, or a disaster such as a fire or flood. These requirements must be defined, built and tested during the project, not left for operations to discover after go-live."],"locked":true}], {"reviewed":"2026-10-07"});
